{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you came in with headache, neck pain, and vomiting?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. I woke up with sharp pain between my shoulder blades and a bad migraine. Then I started vomiting—several times this morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned this feels like the start of an Addisonian crisis? Can you tell me more about that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I’ve had this before—fatigue, nausea, salt cravings. I’ve been increasing my prednisone to 7.5 mg to try to avoid ending up here, but I just didn’t feel right all week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We gave you hydrocortisone in the ER as a stress dose, just to be safe. Your labs don’t show signs of adrenal crisis—your sodium and potassium are normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not an adrenal crisis? But why do I feel so awful?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We think it’s likely a viral illness. You have mild leukocytosis, which can happen with infection or even from the steroids. No fever, and your chest and lungs are clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the neck pain? It’s still really tight on the right side."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You have tenderness along your right trapezius muscle. Given you slept awkwardly last night, it’s likely a muscle spasm. We gave you morphine earlier, and you said the nausea and headache improved?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the headache is much better, and I haven’t vomited since I got here. But the neck is still stiff."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll continue with ibuprofen and clonazepam for the muscle spasm. Your migraine specialist mentioned your neck issues might be related to that car accident earlier this year?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2165-03-20 20:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, exactly. They think it’s all connected—neck strain triggering the migraines."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2165-03-21 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs today show your kidney function is stable, and your hematocrit came down after fluids, which suggests you were a bit dehydrated. Liver enzymes are slightly elevated but improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2165-03-21 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that from the Tylenol I took by accident before? I’ve been avoiding it since."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2165-03-21 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s likely the cause. We’ll keep monitoring, but they’re trending down, so that’s reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve given you the pneumococcal vaccine this morning, and we’re starting you on Bactrim every three days to prevent pneumonia, given your long-term steroid use."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And what about my prednisone? Should I keep it at 7.5 mg?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re going to have you go back to your usual dose of 7.5 mg daily. No taper needed this time. But please follow up with your endocrinologist next week for blood work."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And for the migraines, what should I use now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can use sumatriptan for acute migraines—up to twice a day, but no more than six times a month. And keep using ibuprofen and clonazepam as needed for neck pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. So I’m okay to go home today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your nausea is gone, you’re tolerating food, and your pain is much better. We’ll discharge you shortly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What symptoms should I watch for that would mean I need to come back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "If you develop a fever over 101, severe vomiting, trouble breathing, or feel unusually fatigued, please contact your doctor or return to the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thank you. I’ll follow up with my endocrinologist and take it easy at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll send all the discharge instructions with you, including your updated medication list."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I appreciate all the help. I’m glad it wasn’t another adrenal crisis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2165-03-21 12:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re glad too. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out if anything changes."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2165-03-20 20:06:00",
    "admission_end": "2165-03-21 16:54:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of Addison’s disease, hypothyroidism, and migraines was admitted for headache, neck pain, nausea, and vomiting, concerning for an adrenal crisis. She had recently increased her prednisone dose to 7.5 mg to prevent hospitalization. Evaluation showed stable vital signs, normal electrolytes, and no evidence of adrenal insufficiency. Symptoms were attributed to a viral illness and muscle spasm likely triggered by poor sleep and prior neck injury from a car accident. She received IV fluids, stress-dose hydrocortisone, and pain management. Her nausea resolved, and neck pain improved with medication. Liver enzymes were mildly elevated but trending downward, likely due to prior acetaminophen use. She was given pneumococcal vaccine and started on Bactrim for PCP prophylaxis due to chronic steroid use. She was discharged home in stable condition with instructions to continue her baseline prednisone, use sumatriptan for migraines, and follow up with her endocrinologist.",
    "problems": [
      "Nausea with vomiting",
      "Spasm of muscle",
      "Cervicalgia",
      "Unspecified viral infection",
      "Glucocorticoid deficiency",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Dysthymic disorder",
      "Leukocytosis, unspecified",
      "Migraine, unspecified, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus"
    ]
  }
}
